16 0 obj
<< /A 64 0 R /Alt (Underwater photographic comparison showing two panels. Panel \(a\) features fuzzy and camouflaged objects outlined by red boxes and arrows, while panel \(b\) highlights dense and occluded marine objects within red boxes and arrows, all annotated with descriptive yellow text.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 39 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
17 0 obj
<< /A 65 0 R /Alt (Flowchart diagram illustrating an underwater object detection network. The process integrates depth and RGB images, applies feature extraction, underwater denoise feature fusion \(UDFF\), concatenation, underwater feature enhancement modules \(UFEM\), upsampling, convolution layers, and finally uses D26Head detection heads to generate bounding boxes on sample output images. A legend defines all module abbreviations.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 43 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
18 0 obj
<< /A 66 0 R /Alt (Flowchart diagram illustrating the UDFF neural network module, showing depth input processed by denoising and 1x1 convolution, RGB input through 1x1 convolution, followed by pooling, concatenation, softmax, and further convolution steps, with a legend explaining each operation and symbol.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 44 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
19 0 obj
<< /A 67 0 R /Alt (Schematic diagram illustrating two structures: UFEM \(a\) and UFE_Block \(b\). UFEM processes an input through Conv1x1, Chunk, and multiple UFE_Blocks, followed by concatenation and another Conv1x1. UFE_Block applies Conv1x1, pooling, depthwise convolutions, sigmoid, multiplication, and addition, as described in corresponding legends.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 45 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
20 0 obj
<< /A 68 0 R /Alt (Diagram comparing object detection heads. Panel a shows D26Head with one-to-one and one-to-many heads, denoising, regression and classification blocks, and interactions with grid-based object localization. Panel b details the head architecture, including denoise, convolution layers \(Conv3x3, Conv1x1, DWConv\), and outputs for bounding box loss and classification loss. A legend explains the convolutions and denoising steps.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 46 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
21 0 obj
<< /A 69 0 R /Alt (Four underwater photographs display different marine organisms labeled in yellow text: echinus, starfish, scallop, and holothurian. Each panel features the listed organism in a sandy, green-lit aquatic environment with some rocks and vegetation visible.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 48 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
22 0 obj
<< /A 70 0 R /Alt (Grid of sixteen underwater images in four rows and four columns; each row represents an underwater condition\204density, dim, turbid, and bright\204while each column alternates between original marine life photographs and abstract, color-mapped visualizations illustrating image analysis or segmentation of corresponding scenes, such as fish schools, jellyfish, sea urchins, turtles, and cuttlefish.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 49 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
23 0 obj
<< /A 71 0 R /Alt (Heatmap showing the percentage distribution of underwater target categories across three evaluation scenarios: clear water, bow-light deep water, and dense occlusion. Echinus, holothurian, and starfish have the highest percentages in all scenarios. Darker blue indicates higher percentages.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 50 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
24 0 obj
<< /A 72 0 R /Alt (Three rows and four columns of underwater images comparing detection and heatmap outputs, with each row showing GT, YOLOv11, UMIS-YOLO, and UW-DualDet models; red dashed circles highlight detected marine organisms for ground truth images while heatmaps illustrate predicted regions of interest for each model.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 55 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
25 0 obj
<< /A 73 0 R /Alt (Sixteen underwater images arranged in a grid show marine animals such as turtles, cuttlefish, jellyfish, and fish, some with scuba divers. Colored bounding boxes and confidence scores highlight detected objects, demonstrating an object detection system for aquatic life.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 56 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
26 0 obj
<< /A 74 0 R /Alt (Comparison of three image recognition methods on underwater scenes in three columns labeled \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\). Each row shows original images with manual bounding boxes \(left column\) and two algorithm results \(middle and right columns\) with labeled detected objects and confidence scores, including jellyfish, fish, corals, and cuttlefish.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 56 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
27 0 obj
<< /A 75 0 R /Alt (Collage of underwater scenes featuring marine animals such as jellyfish, cuttlefish, and fish, each outlined with colored rectangles and confidence scores, illustrating object detection and classification in diverse ocean environments.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 57 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
28 0 obj
<< /A 76 0 R /Alt (Three columns compare object detection in underwater scenes using bounding boxes: \(a\) ground truth, \(b\) predictions with some incorrect or missed detections highlighted with dashed red circles, and \(c\) corrected predictions with confidence labels for detected species such as holothurian, echinus, scallop, jellyfish, cuttlefish, and corals.) /K 1 /P 8 0 R /Pg 57 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
29 0 obj
<< /A 77 0 R /Alt (Collage of sixteen underwater photographs and illustrations annotated with labeled bounding boxes identifying marine life such as scallops, starfish, holothurians, fish, echinus, corals, and turtles, demonstrating the application of object detection models in varied underwater environments.) /K 0 /P 8 0 R /Pg 58 0 R /S /Figure /T () >>
endobj
